*  The static keyword in Java is used for memory management primarily.
*  It can be applied to variables, methods, blocks and nested classes.
*  The main concept behind static is that it belongs to the class rather than instances/objects of the class.
*  Static methods cannot use non static data members or cannot call non static method directly.
   To call non static member or method we need to create object of that class and then access them
*  this and super keyword cannot be used in static context
        this → refers to the current object
        super → refers to the current object's parent class
        But a static member belongs to the class, not to any particular object.
*  A static variable is initialized before any instance of the class is created.
        A static variable belongs to the class, not to individual objects. So it is initialized when the class is loaded, before any instance/object of that class is created.
        Example:
        class Student {
            static int count = 10;
            Student() {
                System.out.println("Constructor called");
            }
        }
        If you haven't created any object yet:
        Student.count
        count is already initialized to 10.
*  Class loading → static variables initialized → objects can be created

*  STATIC BLOCK - A static block is a block of code that is executed automatically when the class is loaded, before any object of that class is created.
        Why do we use it?
        A static block is mainly used to initialize static variables or perform some setup that needs to happen once when the class is loaded.
*  Static block → automatically executes during class initialization.
   Static method → executes only when it is called.
*  And main() itself is a static method, but it is specifically called by the JVM as the program's entry point.